title pairing: pools that lose and gain several names at once

# Built blind for the emission cross-check, and the first fixture
# where sorted lost-to-gained pairing is OBSERVABLE. Both
# implementations agreed on sorted order this morning, but every
# fixture until now pairs at most one lost name with one gained one,
# where every rule gives the same answer.
#
# Each pool KEEPS one name. That is not decoration: it is what gives
# the pool an onto contributor and so keeps its dnode. A pool whose
# names all change has no contributor and materializes instead --
# see ok-wholesale-rename.tree, which is that case.
#
#   5-100  keeps /x, loses 2, gains 2   pure pairing, no tail
#   6-100  keeps /m, loses 2, gains 3   one gained left over -> link
#   7-100  keeps /p, loses 3, gains 2   one lost left over -> unlink
#
# The gained names deliberately sort BEFORE the lost ones, so a rule
# that walked either list in the other order would produce identical
# counts and a different plan. Counts cannot see the difference;
# only the edit list can.
#
# Onto is identical to base, so off-of is the only mover and nothing
# conflicts. Tokens never change, so no writes fire and the fixture
# is about pairing alone.

tree base
  1-1: /
    5-100: x = xv0
    5-100: y
    5-100: z
    6-100: m = mv0
    6-100: n
    6-100: o
    7-100: p = pv0
    7-100: q
    7-100: r
    7-100: s

tree onto
  1-1: /
    5-100: x = xv0
    5-100: y
    5-100: z
    6-100: m = mv0
    6-100: n
    6-100: o
    7-100: p = pv0
    7-100: q
    7-100: r
    7-100: s

tree offof
  1-1: /
    5-100: x = xv0
    5-100: aa
    5-100: bb
    6-100: m = mv0
    6-100: cc
    6-100: dd
    6-100: ee
    7-100: p = pv0
    7-100: ff
    7-100: gg
